Because a gasp isn’t only an emergency reflex. It’s also a controllable respiratory action that evolution has wired into several overlapping systems.

An involuntary gasp can be generated when respiratory-control circuits detect a strong need for air or when defensive/startle circuits fire. But humans can recruit much of the same respiratory machinery voluntarily through cortical motor control—just as you can consciously hold your breath even though breathing is normally automatic.

What’s especially interesting in the framework we’ve been discussing is why voluntary access might be useful. A deliberate sharp inhalation can participate in several functions: preparing for exertion or speech, changing lung volume, mimicking or amplifying emotional expression, coordinating with other people, and deliberately altering one’s bodily state. A gasp therefore sits at an interface between automatic regulation and voluntary control.

That’s the fascinating category:

\text{automatic controller}
\;\longleftrightarrow\;
\boxed{\text{shared bodily mechanism}}
\;\longleftrightarrow\;
\text{voluntary controller}

Breathing is unusually obvious because you can watch yourself cross that bridge: forget about breathing and the automatic system continues; attend to it and you can immediately seize substantial control; stop attending and control returns automatically.

And that’s probably why your question jumped out from the preceding discussion. It’s another dual-access interface: one mechanism available from two control regimes.

Gasping makes the boundary especially visible because the same outward action can mean very different things depending on its origin:

body → gasp → conscious awareness

or

conscious intention → gasp → changed bodily state.

So instead of merely asking “why can I voluntarily imitate a reflex?”, the broader biological question is:

Which normally automatic systems expose voluntary handles, and what computational advantage does each handle provide?

Breathing, blinking, swallowing, facial expression, attention, posture, muscle tension and aspects of autonomic regulation all sit at different points along that spectrum.

Those interfaces may be biological bridges between layers of control—exactly the kind of architecture you’ve been hunting for.